  "query_preview": "..A concerning hydrological threat will evolve across\nNorthern Arizona over the next 24 hours with the arrival of an \natmospheric river. Widespread areas will see 1-3 inches, with some\nlocalized areas along/south of the Mogollon Rim up to 4-6 inches.\nHydrological issues will be enhanced due to the already very \nsaturated soil conditions, and it will take less rainfall than \nusual to cause problems. Both flash flooding and river flooding \nwill be a problem, with some area waterways remaining elevated for\ndays beyond the main event. Be prepared in case you need to take \naction or need to head to higher ground. Have a way to receive \nwarnings and always remember, TURN AROUND, DON'T DROWN!\n\nThe deep surge of subtropical moisture has already arrived early \nthis morning. Current mesoanalysis shows a strengthening mid-level \njet and a plume of PWAT values >1 inch moving in from the southwest. \nAlso, current surface observations show south-southwest winds \ngusting as high as 30-40 mph as of 1 AM MST. Widespread light to \nmoderate showers are currently ongoing as a result of these factors. \nThe heaviest rainfall is currently along/south of the Mogollon Rim \nwith help from orographic influences, though periods of moderate \nrain can be expected virtually area wide this morning and into the\ndaytime today. By the late afternoon through evening, a cold \nfront associated with a strong northern stream low will cross the \nregion bringing continued threats of heavy rain and even a \nmarginal severe threat across southern and eastern AZ. Much drier \nair will follow in the wake of the frontal passage. A Flood Watch \nwill remain in effect for all of northern and central Arizona \nthrough Sunday night, though river flooding will likely linger for\nseveral days after. \n\nThere will also be a strong wind threat. Very gusty surface winds\nhave already been noted early this morning, and are forecast to \nstrengthen into the daytime today. A Wind Advisory will go into \neffect today for the Little Colorado River Valley, Eastern \nMogollon Rim, and White Mountains where gusts as high as 40-50 mph\nare possible. Breezy winds up to 30-40 mph are still possible \noutside these regions.\n\nThere is also potential for snow mostly above 7500-8000 ft \nelevation before things fully dry out on Sunday night. The main \narea of concern is the Kaibab Plateau where 1-2 inches of snow are\npossible. A dusting of snow will also be possible for the higher \nelevation locations around the Flagstaff area. Fully dry \nconditions are expected by Monday afternoon and through the week \nas ridging dominates. Another trough will brush past Arizona on \nWednesday, but this looks to remain mostly dry at this time.",
